Imagine you have two friends who each shout out a number whenever they want. `combineLatest` is like a referee who waits until *both* friends have shouted at least once, then shouts the newest pair of numbers every time either friend shouts again.

So if Friend A shouts 1, 2, 3 and Friend B shouts "a" then "b", the referee stays quiet until B shouts "a" (now it has one from each). Then it shouts `(3, a)`. When B shouts "b", it shouts `(3, b)`.

```js
combineLatest([a$, b$]).subscribe(([a, b]) => {
  console.log(a, b);
});
```

Two things to remember:
- Nothing happens until every input has given at least one value.
- Each new value gives you the *latest* from all the others, not the old ones.

It's handy when you need to react only once you have all the pieces, like a name *and* an email before enabling a button.

