Here's a concise JavaScript module that reverses a provided array. It copies the input first, so the original array stays unchanged.

```javascript
function reverseArray(arr) {
  const copy = [...arr];
  let left = 0;
  let right = copy.length - 1;
  while (left < right) {
    const temp = copy[left];
    copy[left] = copy[right];
    copy[right] = temp;
    left++;
    right--;
  }
  return copy;
}

const A = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
console.log(reverseArray(A)); // [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]
console.log(A);               // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] — unchanged
```

The in-place swap loop is clear and efficient: each pair is exchanged once, so it runs in O(n) time and uses O(n) extra space for the copy. If you'd rather mutate the original array directly, call `A.reverse()` — the built-in method does exactly this, concisely and correctly.

